P6-ni 'serpent-day'; VI mM-ni       'horse-day'; VII yG-ni 'sheep-day'; VIII mG\-ni 'monkey-day'; IX }Z\-ni       'chicken-day'; X phN-ni 'dog-day'; XI vA\-ni 'pig-day'; XII fZ\-ni 'rat-      day'.           This corresponds to the Akha order (Lewis 1968, p. 362), which       starts with nyov 'buffalo' as I, and ends with ho 'rodent' as XII [but       Lewis appears to have the glosses reversed for tahv la^ 'mule' (= Lh.       thB-lA 'rabbit') and lahv 'rabbit' (= Lh. l5 'mule')].           However, according to Delmos Jones (1967) the Lahu cycle begins       with lZ-ni and ends with nM-ni (for 'rabbit day' Jones records t5-ni       instead of thB-lA=ni) [cf. thB (Bn)].  The author independently       confirmed this order in a RL village in Aug. 1977.  The cycle is no       longer remembered in any kind of order in X'n villages in Thailand,       which have adopted the 7-day week [cf. PI (N; Clf)], though people are       apparently still named for calendrical animals [cf. cA (Bn), na (Bn)].        See ch^-ni (N) under ch^ (N) 'barking-deer'; also G-lO (N), lO (Mpfx;       Clf).           In the original Chinese system, Rat-Day comes first./     /prob. & c% (V) [q.v.] 'go around'l for the voiced initial, cf. WB      gya{ 'top'; see kh5 (N)/     j%-W  (N)                     ("little j%") the 12-day cycle     j%-lO  (N)                    ("big j%") a 14-day cycle          /precise information on this longer cycle is lacking/     tC-cY-tC-j%  (Elabq)          for a couple of week-cycles; for a while                                    (approx. 2-3 seven-day weeks)     tC j% p@  (NPq)               one full cycle of days     j% p@ ve  (SV)                for a week to run its course          yZ p% ve qhA-n< j% p@ L le /  How many weeks have passed since the                                    child was born?     yZ p% ve j%  (LRC + Nrh)      the w